<title>DENMARK: Danish Jan North Sea oil production falls 1.6 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Jan North Sea oil production falls 1.6 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-02-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields fell 1.6 percent in January 1997 from December 1996, a spokesman for operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Thursday.</p>
<p>He told Reuters that oil production was 965,000 tonnes in January or 228,352 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 232,077 bpd in December.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain. Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p> Field		Jan 97	 Dec 96	 Jan 96</p>
<p> Dan		  69.5	    70.3	   63.0</p>
<p> Kraka		 7.0	     7.3	    7.0</p>
<p> Regnar		0.0	     0.0	    1.1</p>
<p> Gorm		 48.9	    48.9	   38.8</p>
<p> Skjold	     35.2	    34.5	   43.1</p>
<p> Rolf		  4.2	     3.5	    4.5</p>
<p> Dagmar		0.3	     0.4	    0.4</p>
<p> Tyra		 23.7	    24.2	   29.7</p>
<p> Valdemar	    2.8	     3.5	    2.4</p>
<p> Roar		  8.5	     8.0	     -</p>
<p> Svend		28.2	    31.5	     -</p>
<p> Total	     228.4	   232.1	  191.5</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-Chris Follett,Copenhagen newsroom, +45-33-969652</p>
</text>
